WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.952
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.520
MEGAN YAHYA: Welcome to the
Google Cloud Security Showcase,

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.010
a special live
series where we we'll

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:14.100
focus on security use
cases that customers

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.379
can solve with Google Cloud.

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:19.560
My name is Megan Yahya,
and I'm Product Manager

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.060
at Google Cloud.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.270
Today, we'll be walking through
one of the top questions we

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.080
get from customers.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.900
How can I detect
network-based threats

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.510
in my Google Cloud environment?

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:34.440
Cloud IDS is Google Cloud's
network-based threat-detection

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:38.220
technology that detects
malware, spyware,

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:43.050
command-and-control attacks, and
many other classes of exploits.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.500
It provides fully-managed
network threat detection

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:50.610
with high levels of security
efficacy and low noise.

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:55.320
It's quick and easy to
deploy, fully managed, scaled,

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.560
and operated by Google
with high performance

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:01.320
and availability built in.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:05.370
We have partnered with our
friends at Palo Alto Networks

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:09.990
to embed their industry-leading
threat-detection technologies

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:13.020
to make sure we have
industry-leading detection

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:15.840
both in breadth of
coverage and efficacy,

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:19.500
backed by their expert
production-proven technologies

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:23.990
and world-class
threat-intelligence teams.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:27.800
Here is a broad overview
of how Cloud IDS works.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:31.670
Cloud IDS is a fully
managed cloud-native product

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:35.270
that receives the mirrored
traffic from customers' network

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:38.480
and runs threat detection
technology from Palo Alto

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:40.790
Networks under the hood.

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:44.480
Cloud IDS may be applied
via packet mirroring

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:48.920
to any and all traffic
to or from a workload.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:54.380
Whether it's a compute engine
in GCE or a container in GKE,

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:58.700
Cloud IDS moves threat detection
closer to the workload.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:02.570
Not just VPC to
internet or VPC to VPC,

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:06.350
but also intra-VPC
traffic are inspected

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:09.680
for full north-south
and east-west visibility

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:11.360
and detection.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:14.840
This dramatically
simplifies the fulfillment

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:19.670
of the IDS requirement common
to several compliance marks.

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:24.380
It also fills in the east-west
blindspots suffered by most

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:27.350
SOCs in cloud environments.

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:30.770
Cloud IDS also
integrates with SOAR,

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:33.920
which is the Security
Orchestration and Automated

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:39.080
Response solutions, such as Palo
Alto Networks XSOAR product.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:44.090
This enables customers to set
up automated threat responses

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:46.790
based on Cloud IDS's alerts.

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:51.020
We have built integrations
with third-party SIEM solutions

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:53.930
which provide security
information and event

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:56.330
management to our customers.

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:58.820
Leveraging these
partner solutions

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:02.390
in conjunction with
cloud IDS gives customers

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:06.230
additional visibility into
network threats and security

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:09.970
analytics on Cloud IDS alerts.

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:12.440
We are also building
integrations

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:14.690
with Chronicle
security analytics

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:20.870
platform and SCC, the security
management platform on GCP.

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:23.720
Now, let me show
you how you can use

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:26.660
Cloud IDS to detect
network-based threats

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:29.360
in your Google
Cloud environment.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.890
Here is how Cloud IDS looks
like inside GCP environment.

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:37.280
It is located under
Network Security section

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.510
next to Cloud Armor and
SSL policies products.

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:44.210
Once you click on Cloud
IDS, it browse you

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:46.250
to the Dashboard
page, where you can

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:50.300
see a summary of top threats
and IP addresses that have been

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:53.380
detected within your alerts.

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:56.050
You can customize the
dashboard using the time

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:58.870
filters at the top
by selecting the time

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:02.570
period you're interested in.

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:05.240
In order to get
started, you first

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:07.670
need to create
Cloud IDS endpoints.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:14.090
An endpoint is a
cluster of VMs that

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:16.790
are running Palo Alto
Networks threat-detection

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:19.370
technology under the hood.

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:21.980
So first, I enter
the endpoint name.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:28.635
Then enter the network
that you want to inspect.

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:36.310
If you have a shared VPC, this
would be the host product.

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:39.700
Cloud IDS creates a
peered VPC network

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:42.190
attached to the network
you specify here.

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:49.420
The next step allows you to
specify minimum threat severity

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:54.770
alerts you would like to see,
such as Low and above, where

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:59.000
Low is for minor threats with
little impact on organization's

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:02.570
infrastructure, and
Critical is a serious alert.

00:05:06.830 --> 00:05:09.170
Once you create this
endpoint, you're

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:11.420
going to see it
under Endpoints tab.

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:18.940
Now you have an
IDS endpoint that

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:24.040
is ready to receive and inspect
traffic and generate threats,

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:26.230
but it is not
receiving any traffic

00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:31.000
yet until you attach a packet
mirroring policy to it that

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:35.140
will mirror the traffic
from the associated VPC.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:38.665
Click Attach to set up your
packet-mirroring policy.

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:45.480
Now, traffic from
the specified source

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:48.330
is mirrored to the IDS
endpoint for inspection.

00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:53.090
So you give the policy a name.

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:03.895
And you picked the
mirrored sources.

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:10.115
And you press Submit.

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:16.250
The policy is going to show
up next to your endpoint,

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:18.950
such as this policy that
I had created before.

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:23.840
And that's it.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:25.870
Now, you have your
endpoint, and you have

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:27.470
the packet-mirroring policy.

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:30.430
So the traffic is
flowing to the endpoint,

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:32.710
and the detection is happening.

00:06:32.710 --> 00:06:38.260
So you can go to the Threats
tab to see the reported threats.

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:40.420
The Time filter
on top allows you

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:43.640
to view threats for a
specific time period.

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:46.210
You can also use the
Filter menu to filter

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:50.680
by other threat properties,
such as severity or source IP

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:51.310
addresses.

00:06:55.670 --> 00:06:58.340
You can now click
on a specific threat

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:00.610
to get more details
about that threat.

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:08.760
Once you click on
View threat details,

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:11.190
it's going to take you
to Threat Details page.

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:14.190
Threat Details page also
shows the application

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:17.340
based on Application
Identification technology,

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:18.850
App ID.

00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:23.010
It also shows you information
about the CV, severity,

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:28.480
threat type, and more
description about the alert.

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:32.920
If you go back, you can see
that, alternatively, you

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:35.170
can click on View threat logs.

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:43.110
That will take you to a new
tab with threat information

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:45.270
in Cloud Logging.

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:49.020
Cloud Logging also enables
downstream integrations

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:52.230
with your SIEM tools
such as Splunk or SOAR

00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:55.650
tools such as Palo Alto XSOAR.

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:58.380
You can also create
custom workflows

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:02.310
to take remediation action based
on alerts, such as creating

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:05.130
a Cloud Function that
triggers on an alert

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:09.270
and generates a firewall
rule to block the IP address.

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:11.260
Thank you for tuning in.

00:08:11.260 --> 00:08:16.530
Please visit
cloud.google.com/security

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:19.380
for more content from
Google Cloud experts.

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:22.730
[MUSIC PLAYING]

